## Proof

Let $S(x)=\sum_{n\le x}f(n)=Ax^{\alpha}+Bx^{\beta}+O(x^{\delta})$ with $\alpha>\beta>\delta\ge 0$ and $A,B\neq 0$, and let $F(s)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}f(n)n^{-s}$.

### Step 1. A Mellin-transform representation of $F$

For any $N\ge 1$ and any $s$, Abel (partial) summation gives
$$
\sum_{n\le N} f(n)\,n^{-s}=S(N)\,N^{-s}+s\int_{1}^{N}S(x)\,x^{-s-1}\,dx. \tag{1}
$$
Since $S(x)=O(x^{\alpha})$, the boundary term $S(N)N^{-s}=O(N^{\alpha-\operatorname{Re}s})\to 0$ as $N\to\infty$ provided $\operatorname{Re}s>\alpha$, and the integral on the right converges absolutely in the same region. Hence, for $\operatorname{Re}s>\alpha$,
$$
F(s)=s\int_{1}^{\infty}S(x)\,x^{-s-1}\,dx. \tag{2}
$$
In particular $F$ converges (and is holomorphic) in the half-plane $\operatorname{Re}s>\alpha$.

### Step 2. Decomposition and meromorphic continuation

Write $R(x):=S(x)-Ax^{\alpha}-Bx^{\beta}=O(x^{\delta})$. Substituting into (2), for $\operatorname{Re}s>\alpha$,
$$
F(s)=sA\int_{1}^{\infty}x^{\alpha-s-1}\,dx+sB\int_{1}^{\infty}x^{\beta-s-1}\,dx+s\int_{1}^{\infty}R(x)\,x^{-s-1}\,dx. \tag{3}
$$
For $\operatorname{Re}s>\alpha$ both principal integrals converge and evaluate to
$$
\int_{1}^{\infty}x^{\alpha-s-1}\,dx=\frac{1}{s-\alpha},\qquad \int_{1}^{\infty}x^{\beta-s-1}\,dx=\frac{1}{s-\beta}. \tag{4}
$$
Define
$$
E(s):=s\int_{1}^{\infty}R(x)\,x^{-s-1}\,dx. \tag{5}
$$
Since $R(x)=O(x^{\delta})$, the integral in (5) converges absolutely whenever $\operatorname{Re}s>\delta$, and locally uniformly in every half-plane $\operatorname{Re}s\ge\delta+\varepsilon$. Therefore $E(s)$ is **holomorphic** in the half-plane
$$
\mathcal{H}:=\{s:\operatorname{Re}s>\delta\}. \tag{6}
$$

Combining (3)–(5), for $\operatorname{Re}s>\alpha$ we have the identity
$$
F(s)=\frac{sA}{s-\alpha}+\frac{sB}{s-\beta}+E(s). \tag{7}
$$
The right-hand side of (7) is meaningful and meromorphic in all of $\mathcal{H}$: the two rational terms are meromorphic on $\mathbb{C}$ (hence on $\mathcal{H}$), and $E$ is holomorphic on $\mathcal{H}$. Since (7) agrees with $F$ on the non-empty open set $\operatorname{Re}s>\alpha\subset\mathcal{H}$, it furnishes a **meromorphic continuation** of $F$ to $\mathcal{H}$.

### Step 3. Poles and residues in $\mathcal{H}$

Within $\mathcal{H}$ the only possible poles come from the rational terms $\frac{sA}{s-\alpha}$ and $\frac{sB}{s-\beta}$, since $E$ is holomorphic there. Because $\alpha>\beta>\delta\ge 0$, both $\alpha$ and $\beta$ lie in $\mathcal{H}$.

- **At $s=\alpha$:** $\frac{sA}{s-\alpha}$ has a simple pole. Writing $sA=(s-\alpha+\alpha)A=A(s-\alpha)+A\alpha$,
  $$
  \frac{sA}{s-\alpha}=A+\frac{A\alpha}{s-\alpha},
  $$
  so the pole is simple with residue
  $$
  \operatorname{Res}_{s=\alpha}F(s)=A\alpha. \tag{8}
  $$

- **At $s=\beta$:** similarly $\frac{sB}{s-\beta}=B+\frac{B\beta}{s-\beta}$, a simple pole with residue
  $$
  \operatorname{Res}_{s=\beta}F(s)=B\beta. \tag{9}
  $$

No other poles occur in $\mathcal{H}$, because $E$ is holomorphic there and $\alpha\ne\beta$ (so the two rational terms have distinct, and the only, poles).

### Step 4. Maximality of the half-plane

The half-plane $\mathcal{H}=\{\operatorname{Re}s>\delta\}$ is the largest half-plane *guaranteed* by the hypotheses. Indeed, the only information used beyond the two main terms is the bound $R(x)=O(x^{\delta})$, and the integral defining $E(s)$ converges (absolutely) precisely for $\operatorname{Re}s>\delta$. On the line $\operatorname{Re}s=\delta$ convergence is not assured by an $O(x^{\delta})$ estimate, and no information about $R$ is provided that would force convergence or meromorphic behavior beyond $\operatorname{Re}s>\delta$. Thus no strictly larger half-plane can be deduced from the stated hypotheses alone.

(One can construct examples with $R(x)$ oscillating like $x^{\delta}$ for which the continuation has a natural boundary or further singularities on $\operatorname{Re}s=\delta$, confirming that $\mathcal{H}$ is best possible as a *guaranteed* region.)

### Conclusion

$F(s)$ is guaranteed to possess a meromorphic continuation to the half-plane
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}s>\delta,}
$$
and this is the largest such half-plane forced by the hypotheses. In this region $F$ has exactly two poles, both simple:

$$
\boxed{\,s=\alpha\ \text{with residue}\ A\alpha,\qquad s=\beta\ \text{with residue}\ B\beta.\,}
$$

The continuation is given explicitly by
$$
F(s)=\frac{sA}{s-\alpha}+\frac{sB}{s-\beta}+E(s),\qquad E(s)=s\int_{1}^{\infty}\!\Big(S(x)-Ax^{\alpha}-Bx^{\beta}\Big)x^{-s-1}\,dx,
$$
with $E$ holomorphic in $\operatorname{Re}s>\delta$.

